 "drivingQuestion": "What does 'ceiling' mean in Rabbi Yosei's ruling?",
 "outcome": "resolved",
 "outcomeNote": "glosses harmonized",
 "continuesQuestionOf": null,
 "segments": [
  {
   "id": "bava_metzia.117a.5",
   "daf": "117a",
   "sentenceId": 4,
   "he": "רַבִּי יוֹסֵי אוֹמֵר: הַתַּחְתּוֹן נוֹתֵן אֶת הַתִּקְרָה כּוּ׳. מַאי ״תִּקְרָה״? רַבִּי יוֹסֵי בַּר חֲנִינָא אָמַר: קִינִּים וּסְנָאִין. וְ[יוּ]סְטִינִי אָמַר רַבִּי שִׁמְעוֹן בֶּן לָקִישׁ: לְווֹחִים. וְלָא פְּלִיגִי: מָר כִּי אַתְרֵיהּ וּמָר כִּי אַתְרֵיהּ.",
   "en": "§ The mishna teaches: «Rabbi Yosei says: The lower» resident «provides the ceiling» and the upper resident provides the plaster. The Gemara asks: «What» is the word «ceiling» referring to in this context? «Rabbi Yosei bar Ḥanina says: Mats and beams. And» the Sage «Setini says» that «Rabbi Shimon ben Lakish» says: «Wide wooden planks.» The Gemara comments: «And» these two opinions «do not disagree» over the basic halakha; rather, this «Sage» rules «in accordance with» the custom of «his locale and» this «Sage in accordance with» the custom of «his locale,» and they each were describing the necessary materials for a ceiling, according to the local building conventions."
  }
 ],
